# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true in the SYSTEM gitconfig, so on a
# fresh clone every text file is checked out with CRLF whether anyone asked for
# it or not. That is fine for prose and fatal for evidence.
#
# ai-workflow-rules.md: "A criterion that only passes on the machine that
# produced it is a broken criterion." Every committed-fixture criterion in this
# project compares bytes that git would otherwise rewrite between commit and
# clone. Found by Agent A while staging record_sample.jsonl in Phase 0.

# Normalise text in the repository and check it out as LF everywhere, so the
# working tree matches the blob on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Evidence fixtures are read byte for byte by scripts/verify.py. Never let a
# line-ending conversion near one.
tests/fixtures/**   -text

# Belt and braces for the formats that carry evidence, wherever they appear.
# Parquet and SQLite are the dangerous ones: git's text/binary detection is a
# heuristic, and when it guesses wrong it rewrites 0x0A bytes inside the payload.
# That does not fail loudly — it fails weeks later, on someone else's machine,
# as a fixture that will not parse.
*.parquet           binary
*.sqlite            binary
*.sqlite-journal    binary
*.jsonl             -text

# Keep this file itself stable.
.gitattributes      text eol=lf
